From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 01:45:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 150/342] meson_gpio: the header's pins through meson_header;
 periods, edges

The pins and who has them are now meson_header's, so a program's pin,
the I2C bus and the serial port can't take each other's. New ioctls:
pulls; PWM with a period and high time in nanoseconds (84 ns to 0.35 s,
the crystal divided by 1 to 128, at most 65536 counts); information on
every header pin (pad, function, user and team, direction, level, pull)
without a claim; and edges.

PWM: the EF controller takes new counts only from a write that changes
them, and loses a write that follows another one too soon (right after
a clock change, two count writes in a row left the old counts running
at the new clock for good). Writes are now 25 us apart, and counts the
register holds already go in after a value that differs.

Edges come from the GPIO interrupt block's 8 channels (GIC 96 to 103,
edge-triggered): a channel watches one pad, rising, or falling through
its polarity bit; the S905 has no "both edges", so a pin's both take two
channels. Edges are recorded from the moment they're asked for, up to 32
waiting (older ones dropped and counted), and read by a blocking ioctl
with a timeout, which a file's other calls don't wait for. The edge the
switch to a pad can make is dropped. Unused channels are parked on no
pad: at reset they watch the console's TX, level-triggered. On the
ODROID-C2 an edge is recorded 3 us after the write that made it, and
edges 2.6 us apart are all caught.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
